--- body ---
## Context

`/gaze` launches the external panel with a bare `/reviewers request <pr>`.
`reviewers.sh` reads **this harness** by default, so when the reviewed PR
lives in another repository the head branch does not exist where the seats
look, and they die on an unknown pathspec.

`reviewers.sh help` already documents the remedy, and has all along:

> `REVIEWERS_REPO=<path>` — the checkout the seats read (default: this harness)
> `REVIEWERS_PR_BRANCH=<branch>` — skip the forge lookup for the head branch
> Without the first, a branch that lives elsewhere fails as an unknown pathspec.

`/gaze`'s own invocation never passes either. So the harness documents the
requirement in one skill and violates it in the other.

## How it surfaced, and why it stayed hidden

A `/gaze` run on git-erg PR #341 (2026-09-16) reported **zero of three seats
reviewed**. Two independent causes, presenting as one symptom:

1. **This one.** The seats searched `~/.claude` for `t0279-init-rollback-guard`,
   a branch that lives in git-erg.
2. **A dead credential**, not fixable here: `OPENROUTER_API_KEY_IDH` returns
   `401 User not found` from OpenRouter. Verified with a positive control —
   two sibling keys in the same keystore file return 200, so the account, the
   network and the endpoint are all fine and only that key is dead. It needs
   rotating, then renaming to `EXPIRED_*` per that file's own convention.

The panel is fail-open by design, and that is the right default — one seat
must never block a verdict. But fail-open makes a dead seat and a seat that
found nothing **indistinguishable in the verdict**: the run reported no
external findings rather than an error. The failure appeared only in the
`PANEL-INTEGRITY:` lines that ticket 0393 added for exactly this reason, and
those are carried as a note, not as a gate.

The author's ruling (2026-09-16) is that fail-open stays: a run should not
stop because an external provider is down. This ticket therefore removes one
of the two causes rather than changing what a missing panel does.

## Relevant files

- `skills/gaze/SKILL.md` — the panel-extension contract's **How** paragraph,
  which issues the invocation
- `skills/reviewers/reviewers.sh` — `usage_text()`, which documents both
  variables

## Actions

1. State in `/gaze`'s How paragraph that a PR outside `~/.claude` must pass
   `REVIEWERS_REPO`, with `REVIEWERS_PR_BRANCH` when the head branch is known
   — which it is by the time the panel launches.
2. Record the incident inline, so the next reader sees why the variable is
   named rather than reading it as boilerplate.

## Test

None added. This is a contract sentence in a skill body, and the harness has
no test that executes a skill's prose. The honest check is the next `/gaze`
run on an out-of-harness PR: its `PANEL-INTEGRITY:` line should report seats
that ran, or fail for the credential reason alone.

That absence is itself worth noting. A skill instruction that contradicts
another skill's documented requirement went unnoticed until a run lost its
whole panel, and nothing mechanical could have caught it.

## Verification

- [x] `/gaze`'s How paragraph names `REVIEWERS_REPO`
- [x] The remedy matches `reviewers.sh help` verbatim in substance
- [ ] A subsequent out-of-harness `/gaze` run reports seats that actually ran

## Invariants

- Fail-open stays. One seat failing, or the whole panel failing, must never
  block a verdict — author ruling, 2026-09-16.
- `PANEL-INTEGRITY:` lines stay verbatim in the gaze comment. They are the
  only surface on which a silent panel becomes visible.

## Exit criteria

- `/gaze` on a PR outside this harness reaches its seats

## Related

- 0393 — added the `PANEL-INTEGRITY:` reporting that made this visible at all
- 0873 — the earlier instance of the same class: seats silently unauthenticated
  across five runs before anyone noticed
